<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Western Australia</h1><p class="st-meta">1829 CE – 1901 CE · capital Perth</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast western colony that began as a struggling free settlement on the Swan River and was transformed by the gold rushes of the 1890s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fearing French claims, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> established a garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-george-sound">King George Sound</a> in 1826 and in 1829 founded the Swan River Colony under Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-stirling-royal-navy-officer">James Stirling</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> as capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fremantle">Fremantle</a> as port. Unlike <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">New South Wales</a>, it was meant for free settlers, who received land in proportion to the capital they brought.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Poor soils, scattered settlement and labour shortages kept the colony small for decades, and in 1850 it began accepting convicts, about 9,700 of them by 1868. Wool, sandalwood and pearling were important, and pastoralists pushed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal</a> lands in the north. Self-government came only in 1890, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-forrest">John Forrest</a> as premier. The gold discoveries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coolgardie">Coolgardie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalgoorlie">Kalgoorlie</a> in 1892 and 1893 brought a population boom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After hesitating, Western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> voted in 1900 to join the federation and became a state of the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1901. Resentment of federal policies produced a vote for secession in 1933, which <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> declined to act on.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-western-australia.jpg" alt="Colony of Western Australia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Ivor Evans, Leslie John Hawkins, Egbert John Nuttall, Annie Dorrington and William Stevens Vector: Ian Fieggen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Australia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1829 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Perth</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Stirling, John Forrest</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Swan River Colony, Western Australia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=colony-of-western-australia" aria-label="Colony of Western Australia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7663 3340 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Western Australia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7983 3596L4997 651M7983 3596L8774 3709"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M8774 3709h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7983 3596h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7983 3596h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Perth. <a href="/atlas?year=1865&amp;focus=colony-of-western-australia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
